STORY BIBLE v1

Based on FINAL SPINE v1 (Emotional Scarcity World)

A) Premise + Hook

In a society where memories can be legally licensed and repeatedly inhabited, emotional stability has quietly become dependent on shared experiences that were never collectively lived.

When a regulatory auditor discovers that one widely rented comfort memory now functions as psychological infrastructure for thousands of people, she must decide whether preserving truth is worth triggering emotional collapse in those who rely on it to function.

B) Tone Rules

DO

Ground speculative elements in ordinary administrative reality

Treat emotional consequences as measurable, physical, and specific

Let ethical conflict emerge through professional procedure

Keep stakes intimate and observable in real time

Portray comfort as seductive, stabilizing, and sincere

DON’T

No dystopian spectacle or dramatic authoritarian imagery

No villain monologues or secret conspiracies

No technological awe or sensory overload scenes

No grand rebellion narratives

No moral certainty or easy righteousness

C) World Rules (Legal, Economic, Social)

Memories are classified as licensable experiential property with documented emotional profiles.

Ownership is divided into origin rights, licensing rights, and derivative stabilization rights.

High-use memories require periodic regulatory renewal audits.

Licensed memory income is taxable and treated as performance-based revenue.

Emotional impact scores influence market value and insurance eligibility.

Removing a widely used memory can trigger mandated public health monitoring.

Employers may subsidize memory subscriptions as mental health benefits.

Social status subtly tracks access to high-quality emotional experiences.

Individuals can legally lose partial recall of licensed memories depending on contract structure.

Cultural etiquette discourages asking whether a meaningful experience was lived or rented.

D) Memory Tech Rules

Access Mechanics

Users enter full-sensory immersion through regulated neural synchronization.

Emotional intensity is preserved, but interpretive meaning remains personal.

Sessions are time-limited to prevent identity disorientation.

Operational Limits

Only stabilized memories can be licensed repeatedly.

Excessive emotional volatility disqualifies a memory from high-volume use.

Baseline emotional profile must remain within regulatory variance thresholds.

Accumulation Phenomenon

Repeated inhabitation leaves measurable affective residue.

Residue amplifies regulatory stability rather than degrading structure.

System classifies this as reinforcement, not alteration.

Detection

Auditors compare emotional spectral signatures across time.

Micro-latency in affect response indicates layering.

Deep immersion required to perceive experiential drift directly.

Side Effects

Frequent users may experience emotional dependency.

Withdrawal presents as physiological stress dysregulation.

Identity boundary confusion occurs only in extreme exposure.

Black Market

Unlicensed “raw memories” circulate with unstable emotional signatures.

Some markets sell highly specific emotional states rather than experiences.

Illegal edits attempt to remove distress while preserving intensity, often causing fragmentation.

